- [skp-intentions-004] Build Shared-Ownership Structures for AI Production: Nirvana Fallacy — The node implicitly compares the idealized outcomes of collective ownership models against the flawed reality of current corporate structures, without fully accounting for the practical failures and inefficiencies that have historically plagued public or cooperative management of complex, capital-intensive industries.
- [skp-beliefs-002] Observable Labor Market Contraction from AI: Cherry-picking — By explicitly scoping out new job creation and novel employment categories, the node selects only the negative side of the labor market ledger, making systemic contraction appear more severe than aggregate data may support.
- [skp-desires-081] Restructure Economic and Social Systems to Prevent AI from Deepening Inequality: Nirvana fallacy — The node contrasts flawed existing institutions with idealized alternatives (collective ownership, predistributive governance, public data trusts) without rigorously accounting for implementation failures, governance capture, or the practical trade-offs these alternatives carry.
- [skp-intentions-097] Establish Independent Lifecycle Oversight Mechanisms for AI Systems: Appeal to Regulability — The node assumes that because other industries (finance, environment) have been regulated, AI can be similarly governed — without adequately addressing AI's distinctive opacity, rapid iteration cycles, and the difficulty of defining 'compliance' for probabilistic outputs.

